# litecoin.conf — what ltc-replay needs from the node.
#
# Merge these into your existing litecoin.conf and restart litecoind. The relay
# runs on the same machine as the node, so every endpoint below stays on
# loopback; nothing here needs to be reachable from outside the box.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# If your node is pruned, read this first.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `prune` and `txindex` are mutually exclusive in Core. A node started with
# both refuses to start at all:
#
#     Error: Prune mode is incompatible with -txindex.
#
# There is no flag that reconciles them. The consequence is concrete and it is
# the reason this project exists: without txindex, `getrawtransaction <txid>`
# can only answer for transactions in the mempool or owned by a loaded wallet.
# For a confirmed, watch-only deposit it returns
#
#     -5: No such mempool transaction. Use -txindex ... to enable blockchain
#         transaction queries.
#
# — that is, the node stops being able to confirm the deposit at precisely the
# moment the deposit confirms. A backend that reads "not found" as "not real"
# will drop it.
#
# So: leave `prune` exactly as it is. Do not add txindex, and do not reindex.
# The relay keeps its own txid → block index and its own address index from the
# blocks it walks, and serves them on /v1/tx and /v1/address. Set
# TX_INDEX_BLOCKS in the relay's .env deep enough to cover the oldest deposit
# you would ever need to answer for.
#
# One real limit remains, and it is worth knowing: the relay indexes from the
# height it first ran (or START_HEIGHT). Blocks the node has already discarded
# under `prune` cannot be walked by anything, relay included. `getblockchaininfo`
# reports the floor as `pruneheight`, and /v1/tx and /v1/address report their own
# as `indexedFrom`.


# ── ZMQ publishers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The relay subscribes to two topics. `rawtx` carries the serialised
# transaction, which is what makes a deposit decodable the moment it hits the
# mempool; `hashblock` is the wake-up that triggers catch-up.
#
# rawtx and hashtx are different publishers and need different ports. If you
# already run hashtx on 28332, leave it — just make sure LTC_ZMQ_TX_URL in the
# relay's .env points at the *rawtx* port, not that one. Pointing it at hashtx
# delivers 32-byte hashes where the tap expects transactions, and it decodes
# nothing while looking perfectly healthy.
zmqpubrawtx=tcp://127.0.0.1:28334
zmqpubhashblock=tcp://127.0.0.1:28333

# High-water marks. When a topic's queue fills, Core drops frames silently.
# The relay notices — Core's per-topic sequence counter jumps, and the tap logs
# the gap and lets catch-up repair it from blocks — but not dropping them in
# the first place is better. Core's default of 1000 is low for a busy mempool
# and for the burst that follows a new block.
zmqpubrawtxhwm=50000
zmqpubhashblockhwm=10000

# If you also publish hashtx / rawblock / sequence for other consumers, they
# cost nothing and the relay ignores them. Bind them to loopback too.

# ── RPC ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used for catch-up, for block contents on /v1/replay, and for confirmation
# counts. Read methods only.
server=1
rpcbind=127.0.0.1
rpcallowip=127.0.0.1

# Prefer rpcauth over a plaintext rpcpassword. Generate with the rpcauth.py
# helper shipped with Core; it prints the line to paste here and the password
# to put in the relay's .env, and the password itself is never stored on the
# node:
#   rpcauth=ltcreplay:<salt>$<hash>

# ── A note on binding ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# If your config currently has `rpcbind=0.0.0.0` or ZMQ publishers on
# `tcp://0.0.0.0:...`, narrow them. Core's RPC port with `rpcbind=0.0.0.0` and
# no matching `rpcallowip` listens on every interface, and ZMQ PUB has no
# authentication of any kind — anyone who can reach the port gets the live
# feed. Loopback is enough for the relay, which runs on the same host; if some
# other consumer genuinely needs them, bind to the private interface and
# firewall the port to that consumer's address.
